{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1318,1370p' src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"      (set (filter (fn [lease-id]\n                     (or (on-truncated-medium lease-id)\n                         (not (on-any-medium judge lease-id))))\n                   (keys (:ledger judge)))))\n    #{}))\n\n\n(defn- classify-entry\n  \"Step 4: why a lease is due for reclaim, or nil when it is not. The first\n  clause that holds in this order is the cause: already pending, keeping the\n  cause it carries -- a pending lease is never reclassified; released by its\n  holder; tenure start further back than :dao.lease/max, which binds within\n  one ledger lifetime and is moved by no renewal or gap; the grantor's own\n  policy; interval since the last relevant observation exceeding duration\n  plus tolerance, in silence. A lease whose evidence is unknown is due for\n  :silence only once a full duration has passed since its resumed reading;\n  every other condition applies to it unchanged. `suppressed?` bars the\n  :silence clauses only (see `silence-suppressed`), and an unknown entry\n  with no resumed reading is never due for silence -- there is no window to\n  measure, and no malformed ledger entry may throw mid-pass.\"\n  [judge now entry suppressed?]\n  (let [units (:units judge)]\n    (cond\n      (= :pending (get-in entry [:reclaim :state]))\n      (get-in entry [:reclaim :cause])\n\n      (:released? entry)\n      :release\n\n      (and (some? (:max entry))\n           (exceeds? units\n                     (interval units now (:tenure-start entry))\n                     (:max entry)))\n      :cap\n\n      (and (some? (:policy judge))\n           ((:policy judge) entry now))\n      :policy\n\n      (and (not suppressed?)\n           (= :unknown (:evidence entry))\n           (some? (:resumed entry))\n           (exceeds? units\n                     (interval units now (get entry :resumed))\n                     (:duration entry)))\n      :silence\n\n      (and (not suppressed?)\n           (= :known (:evidence entry))\n           (exceeds? units\n                     (interval units now (:last-observation entry))\n                     (duration-plus-tolerance units\n                                              (:duration entry)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"### Q1 — unknown-evidence silence tolerance\n\n**CONCEDE: adopt the conjunction reading.**\n\nFable’s reading is stronger and is required by the contract as a whole. The general `:silence` rule requires the interval since the last relevant observation to exceed duration plus tolerance. The unknown-evidence sentence adds a further necessary condition: a full duration must have elapsed since resumed reading. It does not replace the general condition.\n\nThe anti-speed-up rule at `dao.lease.md:176` is decisive: weaker evidence after a gap may delay silence, but must not accelerate it. Therefore, with duration `10` and tolerance `5`, a gap at `101` cannot move reclaim from `115` to `111`.\n\nAdopt this exact wording:\n\n> “A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for `:silence` only once, in addition, a full duration has passed since its resumed reading.”\n\nThe implementation must require both conditions in the unknown branch:\n\n1. interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus tolerance; and\n2. interval since resumed reading exceeds duration.\n\nAdd a regression test where a gap occurs inside the tolerance window and verify that the lease is not reclaimed early.\n\n### Q2 — holder cap basis\n\n**CONFIRM: accept the flight-time gap, and accept fable’s contract clarification.**\n\nThe cap remains the judge’s tenure-time limit; the holder’s attention bound is measured from its own observation of the grant. The distinction is intentional and consistent with the rule that the bound limits holder attention, while resource fencing supplies exclusion.\n\nAdd these exact sentences under the holder contract:\n\n> “For cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at which it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor judge’s tenure start.”\n\n> “The holder’s observation-flight interval does not extend, delay, or alter the judge’s cap or reclaim decision; a resource requiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.”\n\n### Q3 — served-endpoint latency\n\n**CONFIRM with the corrected premise.**\n\nA serving-path nudge is legitimate in principle; this composition simply wires none. The latency is therefore an accepted composition choice, not a protocol limitation.\n\nUse this exact plan wording:\n\n> “This served-composition first-request latency, up to the configured backoff ceiling (200 ms in the default composition), is accepted as composition policy because this composition wires no serving-path nudge; a composition requiring lower latency must lower its ceiling or provide a serving-path nudge.”\n\nCONSENSUS: Q1 conjunction—retain duration plus tolerance and additionally require a full post-gap duration | Q2 accept flight-time gap, clarify observed-grant cap basis and fencing limit | Q3 accept latency as composition policy where no serving-path nudge is wired"}}
